--TEST--
Spec § 4: `Zone::system()` reads the host, so what is asserted is that it is invariant, that the reader gives it back, and that it is a zone like any other
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Time\Zone::system()` is the one member of § 4 whose answer this suite
// cannot name: it is whatever the host is configured for, and the three CI
// runners, WSL and a developer's box need not agree. So every claim below is a
// *property* asked of that answer rather than a value read off it, and each is
// counted over a sweep so a member answering plausibly once still fails.

final class Reads {
    // Whether the id `VV` printed names a zone the reader accepts *and* that
    // zone is the host's. The one host where the text is not the id is one
    // with no IANA name at all — a bare `TZ=+02:00`, or a Windows zone with no
    // mapping — where `system()` answers the fixed zone it in fact is and `VV`
    // has no name to render, printing `UTC`. That branch is named rather than
    // silently folded in, because it is the only one this cannot hold on.
    public static function idNames(string $id, Core\Time\Zone $sys, Core\Time\Instant $at): bool {
        if (Core\Time\Zone::of($id)->offsetAt($at)->toSeconds() == $sys->offsetAt($at)->toSeconds()) {
            return true;
        }
        return $id == "UTC";
    }
}

// The sweep is over instants, since an offset is a property of a zone *at an
// instant*: a region zone answers differently in January and July, and the
// epoch and a far-future date are where a zone database's own bounds sit.
array<Core\Time\Instant> $sweep = [
    Core\Time::fromIso("1970-01-01T00:00:00Z"),
    Core\Time::fromIso("2024-01-15T12:00:00Z"),
    Core\Time::fromIso("2024-07-15T12:00:00Z"),
    Core\Time::fromIso("2099-12-31T23:59:59Z"),
];
var $sys = Core\Time\Zone::system();
var $probe = Core\Time::fromIso("2024-01-15T12:00:00Z");
string $id = $probe->in($sys)->format("VV");

// 1. Invariance, with repetition as the sweep. `system()` is a reading, so the
//    property that replaces a value is that reading it again changes nothing:
//    sixteen calls, each asked for its offset at all four instants and for the
//    id it renders. A member that consulted the host per call and got a
//    different answer — or that cached the offset rather than the zone, and so
//    stopped tracking a transition — fails the count while every single line
//    it prints still looks right.
int $stable = 0;
int $sameId = 0;
int $c = 0;
for ($c = 0; $c < 16; $c = $c + 1) {
    var $again = Core\Time\Zone::system();
    foreach ($sweep as Core\Time\Instant $at) {
        if ($again->offsetAt($at)->toSeconds() == $sys->offsetAt($at)->toSeconds()) {
            $stable = $stable + 1;
        }
    }
    if ($probe->in($again)->format("VV") == $id) {
        $sameId = $sameId + 1;
    }
}
echo $stable, " of 64, ", $sameId, " of 16|\n";

// 2. The zone reader gives it back. `$d->zone()` re-derives the zone from the
//    `DateTime` rather than carrying the caller's value through, so it is a
//    genuine round trip: converting an instant in the host zone and asking the
//    result what zone it is in answers the host zone, and rebuilding the same
//    reading through *that* is byte-identical down to the offset and the id.
// 3. Whatever the host is, the answer is an ordinary zone: its offset is a
//    whole number of seconds within the ±25:59:59 `Zone::fixed` accepts, which
//    is asserted by handing it to `fixed` — the member that throws on either
//    failure — and getting the same offset back.
int $reader = 0;
int $rebuilt = 0;
int $ordinary = 0;
int $names = 0;
foreach ($sweep as Core\Time\Instant $moment) {
    var $read = $moment->in($sys)->zone();
    if ($read->offsetAt($moment)->toSeconds() == $sys->offsetAt($moment)->toSeconds()) {
        $reader = $reader + 1;
    }
    if ($moment->in($read)->format("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss XXX VV") == $moment->in($sys)->format("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss XXX VV")) {
        $rebuilt = $rebuilt + 1;
    }
    if (Core\Time\Zone::fixed($sys->offsetAt($moment))->offsetAt($moment)->toSeconds() == $sys->offsetAt($moment)->toSeconds()) {
        $ordinary = $ordinary + 1;
    }
    if (Reads::idNames($id, $sys, $moment)) {
        $names = $names + 1;
    }
}
echo $reader, "|", $rebuilt, "|", $ordinary, "|", $names, "|\n";

// 4. It is a value, never an ambient default: there is no
//    `date_default_timezone_set`, so a conversion that named its own zone
//    reads the same on every host — which is the whole reason this member's
//    answer can be unknown here without any other case becoming unstable.
echo $probe->in(Core\Time\Zone::UTC)->format("HH:mm XXX"), "|\n";
--EXPECT--
64 of 64, 16 of 16|
4|4|4|4|
12:00 Z|
